      <p>Rather than opening individual files to look for problems, you can run the project health check from the command line:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"dogsbay-xml project-health . --schematron house-style.sch"} lang="bash" />
      <p>The report covers specific kinds of problems:</p>
      <ul>
      <li>References that point at something missing</li>
      <li>Keys that are used but never defined, or defined and never used</li>
      <li>Topics that are not included in any map</li>
      <li>Files that fail validation against their grammar</li>
      <li>Required metadata the project's policy says a topic must carry</li>
      <li>House rules from <code>house-style.sch</code>, such as every topic needing a <code>shortdesc</code></li>
      </ul>
      <p>The last check needs the <code>--schematron</code> option. A DTD cannot express a rule such as "every topic needs a shortdesc," so the rule lives in a Schematron schema. The health check applies the schema only when you name it.</p>
